---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import PostLayout from '@/layouts/PostLayout.astro';
import Pipeline from '@/components/projects/upose3d/Pipeline.astro';
import CrossView from '@/components/projects/upose3d/CrossView.astro';
import CrissCross from '@/components/projects/upose3d/CrissCross.astro';
import Triangulate from '@/components/projects/upose3d/Triangulate.astro';
import SyntheticData from '@/components/projects/upose3d/SyntheticData.astro';
import BarChart from '@/components/projects/upose3d/BarChart.astro';
import ComputeChart from '@/components/projects/upose3d/ComputeChart.astro';
import { ablations, authors, bibtex, inDistribution, outOfDistribution } from '@/data/projects/upose3d';
import '@/styles/viz.css';

const project = (await getEntry('projects', 'upose3d'))!;
const paperUrl = project.data.url ?? 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.14634';
const officialUrl = 'https://vdavoodnia.github.io/projects/upose3d/';
const themes = { light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' } as const;

const codeCrossView = `def point_cloud(ref, keypoints, cameras):
    """2D point cloud for one joint in reference camera \`ref\` (Sec. 3.2)."""
    cloud = [keypoints[ref]]
    for j, cam in enumerate(cameras):
        if j == ref:
            continue
        F = fundamental_matrix(cameras[ref], cam)     # from the known calibration
        line = F.T @ homogeneous(keypoints[j])        # epipolar line in the reference view
        cloud.append(closest_point(line, keypoints[ref]))
    return normalise_to_bbox(cloud, ref)`;

const code3D = `def estimate_3d(cameras, image_kps, compiler_kps):
    X = dlt(cameras, image_kps)                       # plain triangulation to start
    def nll(X):
        loss = 0
        for cam, a, b in zip(cameras, image_kps, compiler_kps):
            u = cam.project(X)
            loss -= a.log_prob(u) + b.log_prob(u)     # both predicted 2D distributions
        return loss
    return lbfgs(nll, X)                              # no 3D labels involved`;
---
<PostLayout title={project.data.title} description={project.data.description} ogImage="/images/projects/upose3d.jpg" back={{ href: '/#projects', label: 'Projects' }}>
  <div slot="header" class="up-header">
    <p class="up-header__authors">
      {authors.map((a, i) => (
        <>
          {a.url ? <a href={a.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{a.name}</a> : <span class={a.name === 'Saeed Ghorbani' ? 'up-header__me' : ''}>{a.name}</span>}
          {i < authors.length - 1 && ', '}
        </>
      ))}
    </p>
    <p class="up-header__venue">European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV) 2024 · Queen’s University and Ubisoft La Forge</p>
    <ul class="up-header__tags">{project.data.tags.map((t) => <li>{t}</li>)}</ul>
    <p class="up-header__cta">
      <a class="up-header__primary" href={paperUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the paper →</a>
      <a href={officialUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official project page</a>
      <a href="#citation">Cite</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Markerless multi-view motion capture sounds like a solved problem. Put a few calibrated cameras around a person, find each joint
    in every image, and triangulate. In a studio with clean images it works well. The trouble starts when some of the 2D guesses are
    wrong: a hand hidden behind the body, a left foot labelled as the right one, a camera looking from an unusual angle. Plain
    triangulation trusts every camera equally, so one bad view drags the whole joint with it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The usual alternative is to train a network end to end on multi-view video with 3D labels. That works in the setting it was
    trained on, but 3D-labelled video is scarce outside the lab, and such models tend to break in new rooms, with new people or new
    camera layouts.
  </p>
  <p>
    UPose3D<a href="#ref-upose3d" class="cite">[1]</a> is our answer to both problems. It refines each camera's 2D guesses using what
    the other cameras and the neighbouring frames see, it keeps an uncertainty for every keypoint all the way to the 3D estimate, and
    it learns all of this from synthetic data made from motion capture, without any 3D-labelled video. The work was led by Vandad
    Davoodnia at Queen's University and Ubisoft La Forge, and was published at ECCV 2024.
  </p>
  <p>
    This page walks through the method one step at a time, with interactive figures that simulate each idea using real camera
    geometry, then shows what the experiments found. The code snippets are simplified Python sketches rather than the actual
    implementation.
  </p>
  <ul class="toc">
    <li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
    <li><a href="#keypoints">Keypoints With Uncertainty</a></li>
    <li><a href="#cross-view">One Joint, Every Camera</a></li>
    <li><a href="#compiler">The Pose Compiler</a></li>
    <li><a href="#to-3d">From 2D to 3D</a></li>
    <li><a href="#training">Training Without 3D Labels</a></li>
    <li><a href="#results">Results</a></li>
    <li><a href="#limits">Limitations</a></li>
    <li><a href="#citation">Citation</a></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>
  <p>
    UPose3D takes synchronised video from any number of calibrated cameras and outputs the 3D position of every joint. Between the
    two are four steps, and one idea runs through all of them: never throw away how sure you are.
  </p>

  <Pipeline />

  <p>
    A 2D pose estimator first finds each joint in each image, along with an uncertainty. Every camera's guesses are then projected
    onto every other camera's view, giving each joint a small cloud of points. The <strong>pose compiler</strong> reads those clouds
    across neighbouring frames and outputs refined keypoints with their own uncertainties. Finally, both sets of keypoints and
    uncertainties go into an optimisation that finds the most likely 3D position of each joint.
  </p>

  <h2 id="keypoints">Keypoints With Uncertainty</h2>
  <p>
    Most 2D pose estimators predict a heatmap per joint and take its peak. UPose3D instead puts a Residual Log-likelihood Estimation
    (RLE) head<a href="#ref-rle" class="cite">[2]</a> on a standard backbone (CPN on Human3.6M, HRNet-W48 for the out-of-distribution
    experiments). RLE regresses the joint's position directly and, through a small normalising flow, also learns the shape of the
    error around it.
  </p>
  <p>
    So for every joint in every image we get a position, an uncertainty, and a probability density we can evaluate anywhere in the
    image. That last part matters later: it lets the 3D step ask "how likely is it that the joint projects <em>here</em>?" for any
    candidate 3D point, rather than only comparing against a single best guess.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cross-view">One Joint, Every Camera</h2>
  <p>
    The next step brings the other cameras in. With calibrated cameras, a 2D point in one image doesn't pin down a point in another
    image, but it does pin down a line: the <em>epipolar line</em>, where that point's ray would appear from the other camera's
    viewpoint. The true joint must lie on it.
  </p>
  <p>
    For each joint and each reference camera, UPose3D takes every other camera's guess, draws its epipolar line in the reference
    image, and keeps the point on that line closest to the reference camera's own guess. Those points, together with the reference
    guess, form a small 2D point cloud for that joint, normalised by the person's bounding box. Try it below, and then make one camera
    unreliable:
  </p>

  <CrossView />

  <p>
    When the cameras agree, the cloud is a tight cluster around the joint. When one camera is wrong, its point lands visibly far from
    the rest, which is exactly the kind of pattern a network can learn to discount. The step itself is only a few lines:
  </p>
  <Code code={codeCrossView} lang="python" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
  <p>
    Because every other camera adds one more point to the same cloud, the input has the same form whether there are two cameras or
    ten. That is what lets UPose3D handle any number of cameras without retraining, and it is why we call this a scalable cross-view
    fusion.
  </p>

  <h2 id="compiler">The Pose Compiler</h2>
  <p>
    The pose compiler turns these clouds into better keypoints. It has two parts. A <strong>point-cloud encoder</strong>, inspired by
    the Point Cloud Transformer<a href="#ref-pct" class="cite">[3]</a>, turns each joint's cloud into a feature vector. We modified it
    to keep the coordinates, adding a residual connection from the reference view's features to its output.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>spatiotemporal encoder</strong> then looks at those features across all joints and a window of frames (27 in the main
    model) and predicts each joint's refined position and uncertainty, again with an RLE head. Full attention over every joint in
    every frame gets expensive fast, so it uses criss-cross attention<a href="#ref-ccnet" class="cite">[4]</a>: each token attends only
    along its own frame and its own joint, and stacking a few layers lets information reach everywhere.
  </p>

  <CrissCross />

  <p>
    Two things make the compiler useful. It sees time, so a joint that flickers in one frame is pulled back by its neighbours. And it
    sees the clouds, so it can learn which kinds of disagreement mean "this camera is wrong" rather than "the joint moved".
  </p>

  <h2 id="to-3d">From 2D to 3D</h2>
  <p>
    Now we have two sets of 2D keypoints for every joint in every camera: the image branch's and the pose compiler's, each with its
    full predicted distribution. The 3D step looks for the 3D point whose projections are most likely under all of them. It starts
    from a plain triangulation (DLT) and refines it with L-BFGS, maximising the combined likelihood:
  </p>
  <Code code={code3D} lang="python" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
  <p>
    The effect is easiest to see with one bad camera. Plain triangulation weighs every view the same, so the bad one pulls the answer
    away. When each view comes with its uncertainty, a confident view counts for more and an unsure one for less:
  </p>

  <Triangulate />

  <p>
    This is not just a toy effect. In the paper's ablation on Human3.6M, removing uncertainty from both branches raises the error
    from 26.4 mm to 48.1 mm, and the full method's lead on unseen data, shown below, comes largely from discarding unreliable views.
  </p>

  <h2 id="training">Training Without 3D Labels</h2>
  <p>
    The pose compiler only ever sees 2D point clouds, so it doesn't need images or 3D-labelled video to train. Instead, we make
    training data from motion capture. Starting from AMASS<a href="#ref-amass" class="cite">[5]</a>, each sample is built like this:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Vary the person:</strong> add noise to the body shape, mirror the motion half the time, and rotate it randomly.</li>
    <li><strong>Place virtual cameras</strong> on a cylinder around them, at realistic heights, each aimed near the middle of the
      capture volume. Up to 8 cameras per sample.</li>
    <li><strong>Project</strong> the 3D joints into each camera to get perfect 2D keypoints, the targets the compiler learns to
      recover.</li>
    <li><strong>Corrupt</strong> them the way real detectors fail, then build the point clouds from the corrupted keypoints.</li>
  </ul>

  <SyntheticData />

  <p>
    Samples are generated on the fly, so every sample has a fresh random camera layout, and training takes about six hours on a
    single RTX 2080 GPU. Nothing is tied to one dataset's cameras, and the skeleton is just whichever joint regressor is applied to the
    motion capture: 17 joints for the Human3.6M experiments, 133 whole-body joints for the out-of-distribution ones.
  </p>

  <h2 id="results">Results</h2>
  <h3>In the lab: Human3.6M</h3>
  <p>
    Human3.6M<a href="#ref-h36m" class="cite">[6]</a> is the standard multi-view benchmark, with four cameras in one studio. Among
    methods trained with 2D labels only, UPose3D is the most accurate, at 26.4 mm against 30.5 mm for plain triangulation of the same
    2D estimator. Methods trained on Human3.6M's own 3D labels, or with its camera parameters during training, are still ahead here
    (Epipolar Transformers<a href="#ref-epipolar" class="cite">[7]</a> reaches 19.0 mm), which is expected when the test set looks
    exactly like the training set.
  </p>

  <BarChart
    title="Human3.6M: error by training supervision"
    sub="Mean per-joint position error (MPJPE) on the full test set; lower is better"
    caption="From Table 1 of the paper. Rows the paper reports without an MPJPE are left out. The two 'from scratch' rows were trained with no additional data."
    rows={inDistribution}
    max={80}
  />

  <h3>Somewhere new: RICH</h3>
  <p>
    The more telling test is a setting none of the models trained on. RICH<a href="#ref-rich" class="cite">[8]</a> was recorded in
    three new environments (a construction site, a gym and a lecture hall) with different people and cameras. Here UPose3D roughly halves the
    error of the next best method, 34.7 mm against 64.4 mm. AdaFuse<a href="#ref-adafuse" class="cite">[9]</a>, one of the strongest
    methods on Human3.6M, falls apart at 524 mm: it does well only when every view's prediction is reasonable, and occasional noisy
    views cause large triangulation errors. UPose3D's uncertainty lets it set those views aside.
  </p>

  <BarChart
    title="RICH: error in unseen scenes"
    sub="MPJPE on the RICH test set; none of the methods trained on RICH. Lower is better."
    caption="From Table 2 of the paper. Baselines marked as the authors' own implementations in the paper: AdaFuse and both HRNet-W48 baselines."
    rows={outOfDistribution}
    max={80}
  />

  <h3>What matters</h3>
  <p>
    The ablations show which parts do the work. The two branches need each other: without the pose compiler the error rises to
    37.1 mm, and without the image branch it rises to 69.9 mm. Uncertainty is what makes combining them work: remove it everywhere
    and the error jumps to 48.1 mm, and with no uncertainty and no compiler we are back to plain triangulation (30.5 mm).
  </p>

  <BarChart
    title="Ablations on Human3.6M"
    sub="MPJPE with a 27-frame window when parts of UPose3D are removed; lower is better"
    caption="From Table 3 of the paper."
    rows={ablations}
    max={80}
  />

  <h3>Cost, and more cameras</h3>
  <p>
    Because the heavy lifting happens on small point clouds rather than image features, UPose3D is also the cheapest of the
    multi-view methods compared, at roughly half the compute of the next one with both 4 and 10 cameras. In the paper's breakdown,
    the pose compiler needs only 0.385 GFLOPs against 508.5 for the 2D backbone. On CMU Panoptic and HUMBI, adding cameras keeps lowering UPose3D's
    error while plain triangulation and RANSAC level off, and the run time per sample stays roughly constant.
  </p>

  <ComputeChart />

  <h2 id="limits">Limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Calibrated cameras.</strong> The cross-view projection needs each camera's intrinsic and extrinsic parameters.</li>
    <li><strong>Not real time yet.</strong> The paper's focus was accuracy; it suggests learned second-order optimisers for the
      likelihood maximisation as a way to make it fast enough for real-time use.</li>
    <li><strong>Longer windows aren't better.</strong> A 243-frame model did worse than the 27-frame one, probably because the
      synthetic corruptions were tuned for short windows.</li>
    <li><strong>In the lab, 3D supervision still wins.</strong> When training and test data match, methods trained on that
      dataset's 3D labels remain more accurate.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="citation">Citation</h2>
  <p class="cite-text">
    Vandad Davoodnia, Saeed Ghorbani, Marc-André Carbonneau, Alexandre Messier, Ali Etemad.
    <a href={paperUrl}>UPose3D: Uncertainty-Aware 3D Human Pose Estimation with Cross-View and Temporal Cues</a>. ECCV 2024.
  </p>
  <div class="bib">
    <div class="bib__bar">
      <span>BibTeX</span>
      <button type="button" class="bib__copy" data-copy-bib data-bib={bibtex}>Copy</button>
    </div>
    <Code code={bibtex} lang="bibtex" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
  </div>

  <h2 id="references">References</h2>
